panfrost: give a spilling v9 compute shader 4 KiB of scratch per thread

On Valhall v9 a compute shader that runs out of registers keeps its live
values in scratch memory, and once it spills the hardware needs at least
4 KiB of it per thread.  The driver sizes thread-local storage from the size
the shader asks for, which can be smaller, and the job then runs until the
kernel stops it on its timeout.

faux123 hit exactly this on the same GPU generation (Mali-G77 MC9, same
MT6893 SoC): a compute shader in an emulator's opening cinematic ran at
0.2 fps with a job killed every five seconds, 6-7 fps after the floor was
added (no-gain notes, v0.16.0).  Frame time collapses rather than the app
crashing, so it is easy to read as "this driver is slow here".

Only shaders that already use scratch are affected, and only the per-thread
size is clamped, so a shader that needs none still allocates none.  The
allocation is per-thread-size * threads/core * cores, so on this GPU a single
batch that spills takes about 9 MiB instead of a few KiB.

diff --git a/src/panfrost/vulkan/jm/panvk_vX_cmd_dispatch.c b/src/panfrost/vulkan/jm/panvk_vX_cmd_dispatch.c
index 3c1a8d7..9b4e2f1 100644
--- a/src/panfrost/vulkan/jm/panvk_vX_cmd_dispatch.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/vulkan/jm/panvk_vX_cmd_dispatch.c
@@ -44,7 +44,18 @@
 
    panvk_per_arch(cmd_alloc_tls_desc)(cmdbuf, false);
 
-   batch->tlsinfo.tls.size = MAX2(cs->info.tls_size, batch->tlsinfo.tls.size);
+   /* A Valhall v9 compute shader that spills keeps its live values in scratch
+    * memory, and once it spills this generation needs at least 4 KiB of
+    * scratch per thread, more than the shader itself asks for.  Allocating
+    * less makes the job run until the kernel stops it on its timeout, which
+    * shows up as a frame rate collapse rather than a crash.  Shaders that
+    * need no scratch still get none. */
+   unsigned cs_tls_size = cs->info.tls_size;
+#if PAN_ARCH == 9
+   if (cs_tls_size > 0 && cs_tls_size < 4096)
+      cs_tls_size = 4096;
+#endif
+   batch->tlsinfo.tls.size = MAX2(cs_tls_size, batch->tlsinfo.tls.size);
    batch->tlsinfo.wls.size = cs->info.wls_size;
 
    if (batch->tlsinfo.wls.size) {
